# Private paper route

## 1. Scientific objective and author claim

The paper uses electronic-structure calculations to ask whether the allylic hydroperoxide in compound 4 changes the reactant-state electron-density distribution at the nearby C=C bond relative to hydroperoxide-free alkenol 7, thereby providing a plausible electronic rationale for the experimentally observed preference for 6-exo-trig haloetherification. The authors explicitly present this as a reactant-state correlation and caution that it does not by itself establish a reaction mechanism.

## 2. System and model boundary

The modeled systems are neutral, closed-shell (R)-4 (the representative enantiomer of hydroperoxide 4) and neutral, closed-shell alkenol 7. The supplied stationary-point geometries are in implicit aqueous solution. The scored electronic observables are molecular electrostatic potential (MEP) maps and MEP-fitted atomic partial charges, especially the two alkene carbons. No transition state, bromiranium intermediate, solvent molecule, enzyme, or product geometry is part of the computational model.

## 3. Authors' implemented computational route

| Step | Purpose | Input | Method/software | Key parameters | Output | Source evidence |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Relax reactant structures | (R)-4, (S)-4 and 7 starting structures | DFT geometry optimization, Gaussian 16 rev. A.03 | B3LYP/CBSB7; C-PCM implicit aqueous solvent | Optimized stationary points | ev_doc_f59ec506235e_000305_a1febf0b2930; ev_doc_f59ec506235e_000306_d0137e536b8e; ev_doc_f59ec506235e_000307_54d3c6d3b801; ev_doc_f59ec506235e_000308_fd0ad6b73638 |
| 2 | Confirm optimized structures are minima | Optimized structures | Harmonic frequency calculation | Same electronic structure and solvent treatment | Absence of imaginary frequencies | ev_doc_f59ec506235e_000309_5271b88dba87 |
| 3 | Characterize electronic distribution | Validated optimized structures | MEP and fitted-charge calculation; GaussView visualization | Same theory level; electrostatic-potential charge procedures cited in refs. 38–39 | MEP maps and atom-resolved fitted charges | ev_doc_f59ec506235e_000309_5271b88dba87; ev_doc_f59ec506235e_000313_a7436dc81b19 |

## 4. Validation and analysis protocol

The authors checked every optimized stationary point by frequency analysis and required the absence of imaginary frequencies. They compared MEP maps and fitted charges for (R)-4 and 7. The source-backed analysis is that 4 has high electron density near the hydroperoxide and an asymmetric C=C distribution; the alkene carbon nearer the hydroperoxide is more negative and the other is more electron deficient. Compound 7 is described as having a symmetric C=C distribution, consistent with two electronically comparable sites. The authors interpret this only as a plausible influence on subsequent chemistry, not as proof of a specific mechanism.

## 5. Private reference results

Figure S50 contains the atom-resolved fitted charges for all atoms in (R)-4 and 7. The main paper's Figure 4 and discussion report the qualitative comparison: hydroperoxide-associated density is present only for 4; the C=C is polarized in 4 with the hydroperoxide-proximal alkene carbon more negative; and 7 has a symmetric C=C charge/electrostatic distribution. These source-derived values and conclusions are evaluator-private.

## 6. Limitations and interpretation boundaries

The source calculation is a single reported optimized reactant representation in implicit water and does not establish conformational ensemble populations, explicit-solvent effects, transition-state energetics, or causality of the haloetherification mechanism. A benchmark submission may use a different defensible computational route, but must report method, charge definition, conformer coverage, minimum validation, and limitations. Mechanistic statements must remain proportional to reactant-state electronic evidence.
